<p>President Trump said on April 17 that Iran had agreed to let the United States remove the country’s enriched uranium stockpile and bring it &quot;back home to the USA,&quot; describing a possible transfer under a peace deal he said was very close and had &quot;no sticking points&quot;. Iranian officials quickly rejected that account, saying the stockpile would not be transferred &quot;anywhere&quot; and denying that Tehran had agreed to give up its nuclear material. The disagreement shows how far apart Washington and Tehran may still be on one of the most sensitive parts of any nuclear understanding: who controls Iran’s enriched uranium and how any transfer would be verified. Even so, Trump’s repeated public optimism suggests diplomacy is still moving, though the two sides continue to describe the terms in sharply different ways.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/iqsnK90Wa_I5S_lZ5dbcD0Nsyt4XoaJ1J-GZSljFGrS-OUd86UGW-j9hlAJYzBar6dCda_YgLipFQk2r9NVu4KpPuPwaM-C5HPjurGPMjC2Zw6QWedsi_vw59k52ENb-iVgLOEr2W-xNnr8wKf4WmpzaB_TrjyzuNLCYJ4KW8-yNpP3bLECfjFgXO7yXcdjtPwbxUDQ1_KpaOXe3J8MJuKME4k8OBDK7vBYhxAqGCoDK31uWTyCXW8HWGw' alt='President Donald Trump speaks at a Turning Point USA event in Phoenix, Arizona, on April 17, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Excavator plan: NDTV reported that Trump said U.S. and Iranian teams would use excavators to remove uranium from Tehran’s nuclear sites, then transfer the material to U.S. territory under any peace deal.</li><li>Leisurely pace: In comments carried by Reuters via The Straits Times, Trump said the United States would recover the uranium at a &quot;leisurely pace,&quot; referred to &quot;nuclear dust,&quot; and said the retrieval would happen &quot;very soon&quot;.</li><li>Hormuz warning: The Hindu reported that Iran threatened to again close the Strait of Hormuz if the U.S. blockade continues, tying the nuclear dispute to a vital global shipping corridor.</li></ul><blockquote>Agreed to everything - President Donald 